west_total_cost_exact_passing_system_otherwise_rent_borrow_test_keep_the_phone_save_or_defer"],"scenario":"You have taken photos only with a phone for years, but indoor volleyball images often look blurred or noisy. You have about 700 dollars for a first dedicated camera and are tempted to choose between an older interchangeable-lens model with a larger sensor and a newer compact with a smaller sensor. Treat that budget and your phone symptoms as anchors rather than measured results. The frozen shopping set contains an Olympus E-M1 body page at 668 dollars, a Panasonic ZS70 fixed-zoom bundle at 379.08 dollars, a Panasonic LX10 one-inch fixed-lens bundle at 514.87 dollars, a Canon 80D body page at 798 dollars, a Micro Four Thirds 35-100mm f/2.8 lens page at 897.99 dollars and a Canon-compatible 50-100mm f/1.8 lens page at 999 dollars. Frozen model context places the E-M1 in 2013 with a Four Thirds sensor and interchangeable mount, the LX10 in 2016 with a 13.2 by 8.8mm sensor and fixed 24-72mm-equivalent f/1.4-f/2.8 lens, the 80D in 2016 as a DSLR, and the ZS70 row in 2017 with a 1/2.3-type sensor and fixed 24-720mm-equivalent f/3.3-f/6.4 lens. Background material covers sensor format, aperture, exposure time, motion blur, image noise, autofocus and sports-photography equipment. Three community pages contain only scoped questions or author reports about dark-condition settings and phone noise, focus or oversharpening. No source supplies a controlled same-gym comparison, verified current condition and delivered cost, a complete compatible system under 700 dollars, or a universal sensor-size or release-year winner.","target":"Audit what each frozen camera, lens, model, concept and community source can and cannot establish for this first dedicated-camera choice. Build an exact-system table that separates frozen seller assertions from independently mapped model facts and unresolved condition fields; distinguish body-only from fixed-lens bundles; and calculate the compatible lens, accessory and delivered total required to test the 700-dollar ceiling. Explain how sensor format and release year interact with field of view, aperture, shutter time, motion blur, noise, autofocus, burst behavior, lens reach, position and technique without making a one-variable quality ranking. Then design the smallest practical repeated comparison in the same gym, from the same position, for comparable volleyball actions and normalized output size. Record motion-freeze rate, focus-hit rate, noise and retained detail, keeper rate, handling failures and total cost separately. State the exact unit, condition, compatibility, warranty and return checks and all predeclared pass, fail and stop thresholds. End with the lowest-total-cost exact passing system or a rent, borrow, corrected-test, keep-phone, save or deferral outcome, scoped only to the tested gym and units."},"graph_motif":"multi_branch_synthesis","graph_node_to_step_id":{"bridge_autofocus_burst_sports_scope":"B1","bridge_community_phone_settings_scope":"B2","bridge_exposure_motion_noise_tradeoff":"B3","bridge_first_camera_decision_preparation":"B8","bridge_frozen_offer_identity_budget":"B4","bridge_lens_mount_total_system_cost":"B6","bridge_model_release_sensor_mapping":"B5","bridge_same_gym_matched_trial_protocol":"B7","decision_first_camera_pass_fail":"D1","prop_autofocus_scope":"E1","prop_camera_settings_anecdote_scope":"E2","prop_canon_80d_model_scope":"E3","prop_canon_80d_seller_body_scope":"E4","prop_f_number_scope":"E5","prop_image_noise_scope":"E6","prop_iphone_dedicated_anecdote_scope":"E7","prop_iphone_noise_focus_anecdote_scope":"E8","prop_lx10_model_scope":"E9","prop_lx10_seller_bundle_scope":"E10","prop_motion_blur_scope":"E11","prop_olympus_model_scope":"E12","prop_olympus_seller_body_scope":"E13","prop_panasonic_mft_lens_scope":"E14","prop_sensor_format_scope":"E15","prop_shutter_speed_scope":"E16","prop_sigma_canon_lens_scope":"E17","prop_sports_photography_scope":"E18","prop_zs70_mode